| Order marked delivered but missing | Open delivery dispute flow                 |
| Multiple active orders             | Separate order tracking entries            |

The map should display the actual rider location only when location-sharing and order-state rules permit it. Otherwise show a static route or status summary.

# 5. Post-order and retention flows

## Screen 14 — Delivery completion

Show a concise confirmation: “Delivered. Enjoy your meal!”

Primary action: Rate your order.

Secondary action: View receipt.

Optional action: Order again.

Do not immediately force a rating modal over the delivered screen.

## Screen 15 — Rating and review

Two distinct experiences:

- Food and restaurant rating.
- Delivery experience rating.

Use a simple 1–5-star control with optional feedback tags and a short comment field.

States: Not rated, rating selected, feedback entered, submitted, already rated, submission error.

If there is an unresolved delivery complaint, offer support rather than forcing a positive/negative rating first.

## Screen 16 — Order history

Tabs: Active · Past.

Each order card contains restaurant name, date, total, order status and relevant actions.

Actions: Track, View details, Reorder, Receipt, Get help.

Edge cases: Restaurant no longer exists, dish removed, price changed, unavailable item, partial refund, cancelled order, duplicate reorder tap.

Reorder must revalidate every item and price before creating a new basket.

## Screen 17 — Order details and receipt

Display the exact historical transaction, not current menu prices.

Include order ID, timestamp, items and customisations, original fee breakdown, discounts, payment method summary, delivery address, fulfilment status, refunds and receipt.

## Screen 18 — Favourites

Save restaurants and dishes separately.

States: Empty, populated, unavailable favourite, restaurant closed, removed favourite, network failure.

A favourite dish should open its current product configuration, not blindly add an outdated configuration.

