# 12. Prototype interaction contract

A clickable prototype should be testable as a connected product, not a collection of isolated screens.

Every visible interactive element must have a defined result. In particular:

| Component        | Required interaction                                   |
| ---------------- | ------------------------------------------------------ |
| Search           | Opens search, accepts input, displays matching results |
| Food card        | Opens dish details                                     |
| Restaurant card  | Opens restaurant menu                                  |
| Add button       | Validates selections, updates basket                   |
| Quantity control | Recalculates prices and removes at zero                |
| Basket           | Persists items across navigation                       |
| Address selector | Changes delivery destination and revalidates           |
| Payment selector | Updates selected payment method                        |
| Place order      | Validates, submits once, transitions correctly         |
| Tracking card    | Opens corresponding active order                       |
| Reorder          | Builds basket from current menu                        |
| Favourite        | Persists saved status                                  |
| Back button      | Returns without losing valid state                     |
| Error retry      | Retries the failed operation, not the entire journey   |

The prototype should also demonstrate distinct paths for first-time users, returning users, guests, active-order customers and users recovering from a failed payment.

## 13. Quality assurance — probe and re-probe

These are the test journeys I would require before calling Food101's prototype complete.

Test A — Standard purchase

Returning customer opens Home, selects food, customises it, checks out, pays and tracks delivery.

Pass condition: No unnecessary authentication, no lost state, correct totals.

Test B — First-time customer

Guest browses, builds basket, enters address, signs in if required and completes checkout.

Pass condition: Basket survives authentication and address selection.

Test C — Out-of-stock item

Customer adds a dish; the restaurant marks it unavailable before payment.

Pass condition: Checkout blocks invalid item and explains alternatives.

Test D — Failed payment

Customer attempts payment, receives a decline and chooses another method.

Pass condition: No duplicate order or charge, basket preserved.

Test E — Uncertain payment

Customer closes the app during bank transfer or payment verification.

Pass condition: Reopening reconciles status before offering payment again.

Test F — Restaurant rejection

Payment is verified but the restaurant cannot accept the order.

Pass condition: Correct rejection, notification and refund workflow.

Test G — Address change

Customer changes delivery address with items already in basket.

Pass condition: Coverage, delivery fee, ETA and availability recalculated.

Test H — Delivery delay

Rider is reassigned and estimated arrival changes.

Pass condition: Tracking remains accurate and customer is informed.

Test I — Reorder

Customer reorders a previous meal whose price and options have changed.

Pass condition: Updated items and prices shown before checkout.

Test J — Accessibility

Customer uses large text, screen reader and reduced motion.

Pass condition: Controls remain readable, labelled and operable.

## 14. Final design decisions

After challenging the architecture against the main purchase flow and the failure scenarios, these are the decisions I would lock in for Food101 V1.

1. Food first, restaurants second. People usually arrive knowing what they want to eat. The Home screen should let them find dishes immediately, while Explore provides restaurant-level discovery.
2. Four navigation destinations. Home, Explore, AI and Account. Basket and Tracking appear when relevant, avoiding unnecessary permanent navigation items.
3. One checkout screen. Address, delivery timing, payment and totals should remain together. Complex flows are only opened when the customer needs to change something.
4. One restaurant per basket in V1. This avoids multi-restaurant fulfilment complexity until the logistics system supports it properly.
5. Tracking is status-led, not map-led. The customer primarily wants to know where the order stands and when it may arrive. A map supports that information.
6. No fabricated certainty. Estimated times, food availability, ratings, delivery progress and payment confirmation must reflect actual data.
7. Preserve state everywhere. A lost connection, closed app, expired session or failed payment should not force customers to rebuild their orders.
8. One dominant CTA per screen. Secondary actions should be visibly quieter.
9. Promotions must never dominate the purchase experience. The promotional banner in the original design should be compact, contextual and dismissible where appropriate.
10. The design must remain recognisably Food101. White space, authentic food photography, warm orange details, soft-grey cards, black primary actions and vivid green live-delivery feedback.

The single most important success metric is completed orders without unnecessary friction. Measure search-to-item-add, item-add-to-checkout, checkout-to-payment success, payment recovery, and order completion separately. This will reveal whether the interface is actually making food ordering easier rather than merely looking minimal.

For the first prototype, build the 16 P0 screens and their critical states as one connected experience. Then add the P1 operational screens. That produces something you can genuinely test with customers before spending effort on the AI assistant, advanced personalisation and secondary features.